icate, get information, and engage in income-generating activities.\n\nTechnology enabled accounts:\n- Digital financial services, including mobile-enabled accounts have driven growth in account ownership, digital payments, and formal savings.\n- Of the 1.3 billion adults without an account, nearly 900 million people worldwide have a phone but not an account, including 530 million with a smartphone.\n\nFinancial health:\n- About 55% of adults in LMIC’s could come up with extra money in an emergency or cover their expenses for 2 months or longer if they lost their primary source of income.\n- About a quarter of adults in LMIC’s experienced a natural disaster or extreme weather event and over half of them lost income or damage to assets.\n\nOfficial Use Only--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFigure 13: Source of emergency money in 30 days or less, % (standardized to 100 percent) 2024 (https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099022426124530974/pdf/P180967-5a76cfbe-78af-4e4f-b9c4-7f76857fa724.pdf)\nciteturn2425search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; Stacked horizontal bar chart by region and year (2024 and 2021) showing source of emergency money within 30 days or less, standardized to 100 percent. ... Source: Global Findex Database 2025 ... Insurance remains a critical tool for financial resilience, helping households manage risks, recover from shocks, and avoid falling into poverty when faced with unexpected losses.\nFigure 13: Source of emergency money in 30 days or less, % (standardized to 100 percent) 2024\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nStacked horizontal bar chart by region and year (2024 and 2021) showing source of emergency money within 30 days or less, standardized to 100 percent. Regions listed top to bottom: SA, SSA, MENA, LAC, EAP, ECA. Each region has two bars labeled 2024 and 2021. Legend: Savings (dark blue), Family or friends (green), Work (cyan), Loan from a bank, employer, or private lender (orange), Sale of assets (purple), Other (light gray-blue). X-axis runs from 0% to 100% in 10% increments.\n</visual_element>\n\nSource: Global Findex Database 2025\n\n## 2.2.2.3 Insurance\n\nInsurance remains a critical tool for financial resilience, helping households manage risks, recover from shocks, and avoid falling into poverty when faced with unexpected losses. Coverage levels and active participation, however, vary widely across regions. Non-life insurance outpaces life insurance almost everywhere (Figure 14), with the widest gaps in ECA, MENA, and SA, while EAP and LAC show relatively balanced uptake, and SSA have low overall coverage (both life and non-life insurance). --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Canon of American Legal Thought | JSTOR (https://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctv39x51k)\nciteturn2425search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 20.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago;        * “Non-Contractual Relations in Business: A Preliminary Study”: 28 American Sociological Review 55 (1963)\n\n“Non-Contractual Relations in Business: A Preliminary Study”: 28 American Sociological Review 55 (1963) (pp. ... STEWART MACAULAY\n“Property Rules, Liability Rules, and Inalienability: One View of the Cathedral”: 85 Harvard Law Review 1089 (1972) Download XML\n\n     * The Law and Society Movement\n       * “Non-Contractual Relations in Business: A Preliminary Study”: 28 American Sociological Review 55 (1963)\n\n“Non-Contractual Relations in Business: A Preliminary Study”: 28 American Sociological Review 55 (1963) (pp. 445-480)\n\nSTEWART MACAULAY\n\nhttps://doi.org/10.2307/j.ctv39x51k.16\n\nhttps://www.jstor.org/stable/j.ctv39x51k.16\n\nAlthough the professional consciousness illustrated by the legal process materials remained dominant in American legal thinking for a generation after the war, even as it was being articulated legal scholars were at work on alternatives. When the consensus slipped, it would be replaced by an eclectic range of approaches to law and methods for legal scholarship, most of which were incubated during the legal process period, in self-conscious alliance with or rebellion against the paradigm developed by Fuller, Wechsler, and Hart and Sacks. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nStewart Macaulay · University of Wisconsin Law School Digital Repository · University of Wisconsin Law School Digital Repository (https://repository.law.wisc.edu/s/uwlaw/item/4089?page=4&tab=Scholarship)\nciteturn2425search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # Stewart Macaulay ...  | Non-Contractual Relations in Business: A Preliminary Study  | 1963  | Journal Article\n | Justice Traynor and the Law of Contracts  | 1961  | Journal Article\n\n# Stewart Macaulay\n\nUW Law Employee Profile\n\n### Name\n\n  * Stewart Macaulay\n\n### Fuller Name\n\n  * Stewart Macaulay\n\n### Scholarly Profiles\n\n  * Hein Profile\n  * SSRN Profile\n\n### Collections\n\n  * People\n\nImage  | Title  | Date  | Type\n--- | --- | --- | ---\n | Non-Contractual Relations in Business: A Preliminary Study  | 1963  | Journal Article\n | Justice Traynor and the Law of Contracts  | 1961  | Journal Article\n | A Low-Cost Legal Writing Program-- The Wisconsin Experience  | 1959  | Journal Article\n | Restitution in Context  | 1959  | Journal Article\n | The Milwaukee Dispute Mapping Project: A Preliminary Report  | Working Paper\n\n## Contact\n\nUniversity of Wisconsin Law School Law Library\n975 Bascom Mall Madison, WI 53706\n608-262-3394\n\nComments and Questions about the Repository (Elizabeth.manriquez@wisc.edu)--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUKRAINE’S FINANCIAL (https://documents1.worldbank.org/curated/en/099060526050084232/pdf/P512356-01262c4a-b681-49ad-a817-8885c2d3f3c8.pdf)\nciteturn2425search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5 months ago; Source: Global Findex Database 2025 ... up with emergency funds within 30 days, though ... highlighting vulnerabilities in financial resilience; ... Figure 14: Saving or borrowing any money in the past\nand place immense pressure on household finances. In \nsuch times, the ability of households to access reliable \nfinancial resources becomes pivotal for managing \nunexpected expenses and sustaining basic needs.\nFigure 16: Coming up with emergency funds in 30 days \n(%, age 15+), 2024\n8%\n6%\n9%\n18%\n17%\n25%\nMany Ukrainians rely on social networks and income \nfor emergencies, with limited savings and minimal \nuse of formal financial mechanisms. According to the \nlatest Global Findex data, 36 percent of respondents \nwould depend on family or friends for emergency \nfunds, an uptick of 4 percentage points since 2021 \nhighlighting the importance of social networks in \nfinancial resilience (Figure 17). Around 28 percent would \nrely on their work income, showing limited financial \npreparedness for unexpected events. Only 16 percent \nof respondents had savings set aside for emergencies, \nunderscoring a significant gap in financial security and \nresilience. Furthermore, formal financial mechanisms, \nsuch as loans from banks or private lenders, were used \nby less than 10 percent of individuals. The sale of assets \nand other sources accounted for an even smaller share, \nsuggesting that many Ukrainians lack diversified \nfinancial safety nets. \n75%\n76%\nSomewhat difﬁcult or not difﬁcult at all\nVery difﬁcult\nNot possible\n64%\nFigure 14: Saving or borrowing any money in the past \nyear (%, age 15+)\nUKR\nECA\nUpper Middle Income\n60%\nSource: Global Findex Database 2025\n55%\n50%\nFigure 17: Main sources of emergency funds in 30 days \n(%, age 15+)\n45%\n40%\n40%\n35%\n2021\n35%\n30%\n2024\n25%\n30%\n20%\n15%\n25%\n10%\n5%\n0%\n20%\n2014\n2017\n2021\n2024\nSaved any money (%, age 15+)\nBorrowed any money (%, age 15+)\nFamily or \nfriends\nWork\nSavings\nSale of\nassets\nOther\nLoan from a bank, \nemployer,  or private \nl\nd